// The headline parser hazard of gandr-ng9.14, witnessed end to end: an
// application repeating a port name -- `add(x, x)`, one wire consumed twice --
// parses cleanly and reaches the linearity refusal from source. Before the
// member terminator became load-bearing, this shape collapsed into one
// repaired region at the parser and the ruling's diagnostic was unreachable
// from any program, witnessed only by hand-built descriptions.
//
// Two different failures fire, and both reach the user. The name-set fold
// refuses the WIRING: `x` is consumed twice by the frame line, and a wire
// already has that endpoint. The cell layer's single admission seam refuses
// the CELL: the wiring derives the boundary `add(x, x) ==> add(x, x)`, whose
// left-hand side copies the hole `x`, and cell patterns are linear -- the
// ttg ruling's own diagnostic, now triggerable by a program. A copy needs a
// comonoid the type may not have; the respelling names the copy.
sign Copy {
  sort Nat : Type;
  oper add : (l : Nat, r : Nat) --> (s : Nat);

  rule copy2 : (data x : Nat) ==> (z : Nat) {
    node : add(x, x) --> (z);
  };
}

//@ mode: desc
//@ expect-desc-decline: the port name `x` is consumed twice
//@ expect-desc-decline: produced exactly once and consumed exactly once
//@ expect-desc-cell-decline: non-linear cell pattern
//@ expect-desc-cell-decline: the producer hole `x` occurs more than once
//@ expect-desc-composites: 0
